  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 6♥. West's lead is won by South. South has a loser in spades and a possible trump loser if hearts are 4-1. In this case, South will need the double ruffing finesse in diamonds to get rid of his spade loser.\n\nSouth should begin by cashing a top heart in hand before crossing to the ♥A. If both defenders follow, a diamond can be ruffed. The last trump can be drawn and a spade trick will be lost eventually.\n\nIf a defender discards on the second round of trump, South must build a diamond trick. East will have to have both the ♦A and ♦K.\n\nA diamond is played from dummy, covered by East, and ruffed by South. The dummy is entered in clubs for a second diamond play, again covered and ruffed. Dummy will have an established diamond trick to take care of South's ♠2.\n\nIn case the defender with the long hearts also has a singleton club, South should cash his other top heart and concede a trump trick to the defense. South can later cross in clubs to enjoy dummy's diamond winner.",
